[[:db/retract 17592186174160 :answer-field/choices 17592186174161] [:db/retract 17592186174160 :answer-field/choices 17592186174162] [:db/retract 17592186174160 :answer-field/choices 17592186174163] [:db/retract 17592186174160 :answer-field/choices 17592186174164] [:db/retract 17592186174160 :answer-field/choices 17592186174165] {:db/id 17592186176090 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c75ad058-938d-576a-bc4e-215aa43fed14" "question-f6527ad8-2bed-55da-aea0-3bbb3266c020"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175716 :question/problem "Evaluate $2^{-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $({\\color{blue}{2}}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $({{\\color{red}{+3}}}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1}{2\\times 2\\times 2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176091 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-52bff138-3308-56fb-8e00-4b3a80e44a0d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175717 :question/problem "Calculate the value of $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $\\left(\\dfrac 1 4\\right),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+{\\color{blue}2}),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{-2} & =(4)^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176092 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4de46f6f-1e6b-5ab8-947e-dc550a3db276"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175718 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\dfrac{1}{49}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{49} & =\\frac{1\\times 1}{7\\times 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{7}\\right),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{1}{7} \\right)^2 = 7^{-2}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c75ad058-938d-576a-bc4e-215aa43fed14" :question/id #uuid "c75ad058-938d-576a-bc4e-215aa43fed14" :question/math-academy-id "q-175985" :question/problem "Expressed as a fraction in its simplest form, ${4}^{-4}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(4),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{-4} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1\\times 1}{4\\times 4\\times 4\\times 4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68579504-4350-520d-a0e5-3de9612d9cff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68579504-4350-520d-a0e5-3de9612d9cff" :answer-field/id #uuid "68579504-4350-520d-a0e5-3de9612d9cff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3006a724-b763-5eca-a3b5-b0cae30c9023" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3006a724-b763-5eca-a3b5-b0cae30c9023"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3006a724-b763-5eca-a3b5-b0cae30c9023" :answer/id #uuid "3006a724-b763-5eca-a3b5-b0cae30c9023" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{256}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f6527ad8-2bed-55da-aea0-3bbb3266c020" :question/id #uuid "f6527ad8-2bed-55da-aea0-3bbb3266c020" :question/math-academy-id "q-6197" :question/problem "${4}^{-3}=$" :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(4),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{-3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4})\\times (\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1\\times 1}{4\\times 4\\times 4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-984a702d-477d-57e0-aacd-910dcf28c310"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-984a702d-477d-57e0-aacd-910dcf28c310" :answer-field/id #uuid "984a702d-477d-57e0-aacd-910dcf28c310" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4cd74255-cb9f-5d8c-835b-d4b5716f3ea4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4754cd8-29db-546d-bbf6-c0e4fedfb324" "answer-bc4b3278-a327-5d85-aa9d-e9d61be3a1c9" "answer-98d7d377-a710-5f5a-88d4-bc89948036ea" "answer-58dafc0d-d970-5fdf-bd4d-323f6d360781" "answer-4cd74255-cb9f-5d8c-835b-d4b5716f3ea4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4754cd8-29db-546d-bbf6-c0e4fedfb324" :answer/id #uuid "d4754cd8-29db-546d-bbf6-c0e4fedfb324" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc4b3278-a327-5d85-aa9d-e9d61be3a1c9" :answer/id #uuid "bc4b3278-a327-5d85-aa9d-e9d61be3a1c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98d7d377-a710-5f5a-88d4-bc89948036ea" :answer/id #uuid "98d7d377-a710-5f5a-88d4-bc89948036ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58dafc0d-d970-5fdf-bd4d-323f6d360781" :answer/id #uuid "58dafc0d-d970-5fdf-bd4d-323f6d360781" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cd74255-cb9f-5d8c-835b-d4b5716f3ea4" :answer/id #uuid "4cd74255-cb9f-5d8c-835b-d4b5716f3ea4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-52bff138-3308-56fb-8e00-4b3a80e44a0d" :question/id #uuid "52bff138-3308-56fb-8e00-4b3a80e44a0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-173748" :question/problem "Expressed as a whole number or fraction in its simplest form, ${(\\frac{1}{10})}^{-3}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{10}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{-3} & =(10)^{3} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 10\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =1,000\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c042d8d-57b0-5cf2-82ee-696d9e75dc07"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c042d8d-57b0-5cf2-82ee-696d9e75dc07" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c042d8d-57b0-5cf2-82ee-696d9e75dc07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ba2f1956-9c7f-5c6f-b22c-f0da7d94a10f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba2f1956-9c7f-5c6f-b22c-f0da7d94a10f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba2f1956-9c7f-5c6f-b22c-f0da7d94a10f" :answer/id #uuid "ba2f1956-9c7f-5c6f-b22c-f0da7d94a10f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176488 :question/problem "${(\\frac{1}{9})}^{-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{1}{9}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(+2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{-2} & =(9)^{2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =81\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175066 :answer-field/correct 17592186175069 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176317 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{9}{4}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{2\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{3}{2}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}={(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174160 :answer-field/correct "answer-7c2fd2a0-35b0-5c42-b70a-f84a88d2104e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fd7d8a12-8d4e-52c1-ba72-0806b3d500b1" "answer-9ddc2f7c-ba78-53a1-9db1-6d1f0654ddad" "answer-7c2fd2a0-35b0-5c42-b70a-f84a88d2104e" "answer-41f1fafd-d827-531f-8c70-6919b2012ba0" "answer-5c52ccdf-bc66-5508-a679-0e1f1417b58e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd7d8a12-8d4e-52c1-ba72-0806b3d500b1" :answer/id #uuid "fd7d8a12-8d4e-52c1-ba72-0806b3d500b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ddc2f7c-ba78-53a1-9db1-6d1f0654ddad" :answer/id #uuid "9ddc2f7c-ba78-53a1-9db1-6d1f0654ddad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c2fd2a0-35b0-5c42-b70a-f84a88d2104e" :answer/id #uuid "7c2fd2a0-35b0-5c42-b70a-f84a88d2104e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41f1fafd-d827-531f-8c70-6919b2012ba0" :answer/id #uuid "41f1fafd-d827-531f-8c70-6919b2012ba0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c52ccdf-bc66-5508-a679-0e1f1417b58e" :answer/id #uuid "5c52ccdf-bc66-5508-a679-0e1f1417b58e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4de46f6f-1e6b-5ab8-947e-dc550a3db276" :question/id #uuid "4de46f6f-1e6b-5ab8-947e-dc550a3db276" :question/math-academy-id "q-128737" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{36}{25}$ as a base raised to a negative exponent." :question/worked-solution "First, we write $\\frac{36}{25}$ as a base raised to a power:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36}{25} & =\\frac{6\\times 6}{5\\times 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}\\times \\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the reciprocal of the base $(\\frac{6}{5}),$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $(-2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{-2}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f528d313-aed4-573c-9b07-3ba92a86a40f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f528d313-aed4-573c-9b07-3ba92a86a40f" :answer-field/id #uuid "f528d313-aed4-573c-9b07-3ba92a86a40f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3635da9f-6e9e-5c6c-b0df-8b6493466f48" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c29dd532-663b-5c61-a8e3-572bc60ac813" "answer-2638936c-5b0f-58a4-994e-0d8105ed344e" "answer-3635da9f-6e9e-5c6c-b0df-8b6493466f48" "answer-83290b9d-c95c-5ea2-a4b2-df6ea439c113" "answer-7ce93af7-3222-5d51-a2a5-1849488558cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c29dd532-663b-5c61-a8e3-572bc60ac813" :answer/id #uuid "c29dd532-663b-5c61-a8e3-572bc60ac813" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2638936c-5b0f-58a4-994e-0d8105ed344e" :answer/id #uuid "2638936c-5b0f-58a4-994e-0d8105ed344e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3635da9f-6e9e-5c6c-b0df-8b6493466f48" :answer/id #uuid "3635da9f-6e9e-5c6c-b0df-8b6493466f48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83290b9d-c95c-5ea2-a4b2-df6ea439c113" :answer/id #uuid "83290b9d-c95c-5ea2-a4b2-df6ea439c113" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ce93af7-3222-5d51-a2a5-1849488558cf" :answer/id #uuid "7ce93af7-3222-5d51-a2a5-1849488558cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{(\\frac{6}{5})}^{-2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177087 :tutorial/content "We know how to find $2^{-1}.$ But what about $2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots$?\n\nTo work out ${\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}},$ all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base $({\\color{blue}{2}})$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $({{\\color{red}{+2}}})$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1}{2\\times 2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176112 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186316731 :answer-field/choices 17592186316512] {:db/id 17592186166960 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac1dfb2b-dc39-5a8f-bb78-6916e280fb3b" "question-967d4b0f-c5b8-576b-b414-bb183f8a1110"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167292 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ -{x}^{2}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac1dfb2b-dc39-5a8f-bb78-6916e280fb3b" :question/id #uuid "ac1dfb2b-dc39-5a8f-bb78-6916e280fb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-343361" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ x+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=-3.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-3.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{-}}f(x)=4 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}f(x)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-3)+{f}^{+}(-3)}{2}=\\frac{4+(-2)}{2}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e653195-c3d5-582e-822a-b0c443f4aade"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e653195-c3d5-582e-822a-b0c443f4aade" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e653195-c3d5-582e-822a-b0c443f4aade" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-857250c9-a9b1-5080-b2c5-1728296672ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-857250c9-a9b1-5080-b2c5-1728296672ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-857250c9-a9b1-5080-b2c5-1728296672ed" :answer/id #uuid "857250c9-a9b1-5080-b2c5-1728296672ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-967d4b0f-c5b8-576b-b414-bb183f8a1110" :question/id #uuid "967d4b0f-c5b8-576b-b414-bb183f8a1110" :question/math-academy-id "q-343368" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-3, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0 \\\\ {f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{f}^{+}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2}=\\frac{0+(-3)}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-539ad74a-5113-59bb-99ce-f5b195bb6b58"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-539ad74a-5113-59bb-99ce-f5b195bb6b58" :answer-field/id #uuid "539ad74a-5113-59bb-99ce-f5b195bb6b58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-752a3ed9-4c29-5165-ac74-445a7667e23c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-752a3ed9-4c29-5165-ac74-445a7667e23c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-752a3ed9-4c29-5165-ac74-445a7667e23c" :answer/id #uuid "752a3ed9-4c29-5165-ac74-445a7667e23c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375037 :question/problem "Consider the function $F(x)=|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{2}{\\pi }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (4{n}^{2}-1)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2nx).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2n\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4{n}^{2}-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317402 :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ -5, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186316731 :answer-field/correct 17592186315567 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105916 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b93ffaa5-a268-5040-8e2a-c610ae7ae03c" "question-4f10acf7-191a-5a7b-ad4b-e4a80fb0c241"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105490 :question/problem "Let ![](images/bf/bf51b89da6e28b6f6cf620b709298b81b8c202b54a601741b0bed05cd265b3e9.svg) and ![](images/39/39895d1db9365c174ca95dcbd4ba9d374a1c14929859f652e3cfdd07993569ac.svg) Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b93ffaa5-a268-5040-8e2a-c610ae7ae03c" :question/id #uuid "b93ffaa5-a268-5040-8e2a-c610ae7ae03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-275242" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=7$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={90}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d844d6a-b86e-5583-9197-f2e6b196cc47"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d844d6a-b86e-5583-9197-f2e6b196cc47" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d844d6a-b86e-5583-9197-f2e6b196cc47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-28e79a2a-f934-5d02-bb33-fed7a4c61fee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-28e79a2a-f934-5d02-bb33-fed7a4c61fee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28e79a2a-f934-5d02-bb33-fed7a4c61fee" :answer/id #uuid "28e79a2a-f934-5d02-bb33-fed7a4c61fee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f10acf7-191a-5a7b-ad4b-e4a80fb0c241" :question/id #uuid "4f10acf7-191a-5a7b-ad4b-e4a80fb0c241" :question/math-academy-id "q-101521" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=6\\sqrt{3}$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta ={30}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e3aefe0-abfb-587a-90a2-c75247bc2342"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e3aefe0-abfb-587a-90a2-c75247bc2342" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e3aefe0-abfb-587a-90a2-c75247bc2342" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-31a44f88-a9d2-5ac1-9074-4eaba651ac12" :answer-field/choices ["answer-311f9824-699a-5a38-83d3-40267dc46ab5" "answer-9f74324e-554a-5e36-8177-d8ee9a8eb1b0" "answer-e8ca569d-59b2-52ea-96f3-06e9ff60d259" "answer-ce889001-65f4-5414-8ef9-dfcfe539055e" "answer-31a44f88-a9d2-5ac1-9074-4eaba651ac12"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-311f9824-699a-5a38-83d3-40267dc46ab5" :answer/id #uuid "311f9824-699a-5a38-83d3-40267dc46ab5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f74324e-554a-5e36-8177-d8ee9a8eb1b0" :answer/id #uuid "9f74324e-554a-5e36-8177-d8ee9a8eb1b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8ca569d-59b2-52ea-96f3-06e9ff60d259" :answer/id #uuid "e8ca569d-59b2-52ea-96f3-06e9ff60d259" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce889001-65f4-5414-8ef9-dfcfe539055e" :answer/id #uuid "ce889001-65f4-5414-8ef9-dfcfe539055e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31a44f88-a9d2-5ac1-9074-4eaba651ac12" :answer/id #uuid "31a44f88-a9d2-5ac1-9074-4eaba651ac12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107174 :tutorial/content "The **scalar projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the distance that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2$ and that the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nTo illustrate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b},$ consider the point $P$ on the line that contains $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fd/fd37bdb2a97b4125fb360990d3c4a36b48bcb50433de033a154799db00e474dd.png)\n\nThe length $OP$ is called the **scalar projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or the **component** of $\\mathbf{a}$ along $\\mathbf{b}$). It is denoted as $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and we can compute it using cosine in $\\triangle OPA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =OP \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis formula is the general definition of the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107177 :tutorial/content "The scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ can be negative. This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c19648ea2df1935e89e10df2f7f5ab33ad537a5fdb52bb97c3555c9a0365701e.png)\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2, \\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNotice that the angle $\\theta$ between the vectors is obtuse. \n\nComputing the scalar projection of $\\mathbf a$ onto $\\mathbf b$ using the formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if $90^\\circ < \\theta \\leq 180^\\circ,$ then $\\cos\\theta <0$ and the scalar projection will be negative." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107175 :tutorial/content "We have been using the following formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}= |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nHowever, sometimes we are not given the angle between two vectors. In such cases, we can solve for $\\cos \\theta$ in terms of the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & =|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our original formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =|\\,a\\,|\\cdot \\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,|\\,|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105962 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064341 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f002498-444e-5f1e-b174-d456ccc0407b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f002498-444e-5f1e-b174-d456ccc0407b" :question/id #uuid "4f002498-444e-5f1e-b174-d456ccc0407b" :question/math-academy-id "q-133261" :question/problem "Expressed as a polynomial in terms of $x,$ the length of Fred's plot of land is{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "The width of the plot is $x+3$ meters, and its area is $2{x}^{2}+6x$ square meters. Since the $\\text{area}=\\text{length}\\times \\text{width},$ we factor the expression for the area to find the length.\n\nFirst, we find the $\\text{GCF}$ of the two terms:\n\n$$\n\\text{GCF}(2{x}^{2},6x)=2x\n$$\n\nThen, we rewrite each term in the original expression as a product with the $\\text{GCF}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+6x & = \\\\ (2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we write the $\\text{GCF}$ outside the parentheses and keep the remaining terms inside:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x\\cdot x)+(2x\\cdot 3) & = \\\\ 2x(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $2x$ meters.\n\n![](images/19/19307236885ccd9bc41d9bdd70df230932f2858804a24e96945880124dee0457.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-85424d4a-06a8-54a2-8057-3520a4258478"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-85424d4a-06a8-54a2-8057-3520a4258478" :answer-field/id #uuid "85424d4a-06a8-54a2-8057-3520a4258478" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ba0f05c3-c538-51c4-9189-cfab53990b09" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ba0f05c3-c538-51c4-9189-cfab53990b09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba0f05c3-c538-51c4-9189-cfab53990b09" :answer/id #uuid "ba0f05c3-c538-51c4-9189-cfab53990b09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064328 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6574f685-c760-598c-909a-a94bd6f0ab34"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6574f685-c760-598c-909a-a94bd6f0ab34" :question/id #uuid "6574f685-c760-598c-909a-a94bd6f0ab34" :question/math-academy-id "q-133263" :question/problem "![](images/2e/2ee258ce570d5afc7a014eece4d759c074fbc2dc618fea1eccf26be502ed6e0d.png)\n\nDebbie is Fred's neighbor. She has a plot of land whose area is ${x}^{2}-9$ square meters and whose width is $x+3$ meters, as depicted above. What is the length of Debbie's plot in terms of $x?$\n\nDebbie's plot has a length of\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "To find the length of Debbie's plot of land, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}-9.$\n\nFirst, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-9={x}^{2}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{3}^{2}=(x+3)(x-3)\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the length of the plot must be $x-3$ meters.\n\n![](images/fe/fec51af6e1d422bb55951e72a328c9b6d7555197303349bbbec9352434aac196.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c38c907-b67d-5f29-a20f-e20d706339a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c38c907-b67d-5f29-a20f-e20d706339a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c38c907-b67d-5f29-a20f-e20d706339a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7e0f5dbb-1d95-58b7-a499-8c1c9edd9437" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7e0f5dbb-1d95-58b7-a499-8c1c9edd9437"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e0f5dbb-1d95-58b7-a499-8c1c9edd9437" :answer/id #uuid "7e0f5dbb-1d95-58b7-a499-8c1c9edd9437" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054775 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1a7f0062-c2c7-5c0c-af15-d14b42ae0b4e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1a7f0062-c2c7-5c0c-af15-d14b42ae0b4e" :question/id #uuid "1a7f0062-c2c7-5c0c-af15-d14b42ae0b4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-133269" :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a3abcc5987a334da234faca33d5d906fde8b1482631e7a4bf281b2094c2339b.png)\n\nA utility pole is located on the edge of Fred's land, as shown above. The distance from the right edge of Fred's plot to the pole is $x-5$ meters. Find the distance to the pole from the left edge as a function of $x.$\n\nThe required distance is \n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that the length of Fred's plot is $2x$ meters.\n\nTo find the required distance, we need to subtract polynomials $2x$ and $x-5:$\n\nWe put a minus sign between the two polynomials, distribute it over the second polynomial, collect like terms, and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x)-(x-5) & = \\\\ 2x-x+5 & = \\\\ (2-1)x+5 & = \\\\ x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-17348961-19d5-502d-926a-4b0fe905626b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-17348961-19d5-502d-926a-4b0fe905626b" :answer-field/id #uuid "17348961-19d5-502d-926a-4b0fe905626b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-77e5bd0e-3c61-550f-bf29-e8fd2b0a9051" :answer-field/choices ["answer-77e5bd0e-3c61-550f-bf29-e8fd2b0a9051"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77e5bd0e-3c61-550f-bf29-e8fd2b0a9051" :answer/id #uuid "77e5bd0e-3c61-550f-bf29-e8fd2b0a9051" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054659 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e97c6917-f66f-5f90-bc2c-151969a83727"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e97c6917-f66f-5f90-bc2c-151969a83727" :question/id #uuid "e97c6917-f66f-5f90-bc2c-151969a83727" :question/math-academy-id "q-133265" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5bbf82f97549272635ff1ea78d945691cf356dfbde2d0933788f734d6b7f9601.png)\n\nFred's neighbor to the south, Emma, has a land plot of area ${x}^{2}+3x-10$ square meters, as shown in the picture. Given that the length of Emma's plot of land is $x+5$ meters, what is the width of the plot in terms of $x?$\n\nEmma's plot width is\n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "To find the required width, we need to factor the expression ${x}^{2}+3x-10.$\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-10).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative, and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-10) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 10 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-5) \\\\ (-2) & \\times 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-10) & =-9 & \\times  \\\\ -1+10 & =9 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-5) & =-3 & \\times  \\\\ -2+5 & =3 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, write the linear term as $3x=5x-2x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x-10 & ={x}^{2}+5x-2x-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately, and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-2x-10 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+5x)+(-2x-10) & = \\\\ x(x+5)-2(x+5) & = \\\\ (x-2)(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the width must be $x-2$ meters. \n\n![](images/6b/6bdc252e1952c6e56a971c7d88c126a75a68e3ae48783c7cb65495ed5b4b86b1.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-df1e0c5e-89c3-5935-b671-11830fe30345"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-df1e0c5e-89c3-5935-b671-11830fe30345" :answer-field/id #uuid "df1e0c5e-89c3-5935-b671-11830fe30345" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9f967a28-837b-54d2-af49-03c005191815" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9f967a28-837b-54d2-af49-03c005191815"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f967a28-837b-54d2-af49-03c005191815" :answer/id #uuid "9f967a28-837b-54d2-af49-03c005191815" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078472 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-166cb87b-3c73-5b03-8260-c56aff73aa94"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-166cb87b-3c73-5b03-8260-c56aff73aa94" :question/id #uuid "166cb87b-3c73-5b03-8260-c56aff73aa94" :question/math-academy-id "q-133406" :question/problem "What is the degree of the polynomial that represents the area of Emma's plot of land?\n\nDegree $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The polynomial \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3x-10\n$$\n\ncan be written as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+3{x}^{1}-10{x}^{0}.\n$$\n\nSo, it has the exponents $2,$ $1,$ and $0.$ The degree is the largest of these exponents, which is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-db3c9891-1eda-5afe-bfcf-4f6138888c67"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-db3c9891-1eda-5afe-bfcf-4f6138888c67" :answer-field/id #uuid "db3c9891-1eda-5afe-bfcf-4f6138888c67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a7be5285-b7c0-5f78-a34f-88bd916c25e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a7be5285-b7c0-5f78-a34f-88bd916c25e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7be5285-b7c0-5f78-a34f-88bd916c25e2" :answer/id #uuid "a7be5285-b7c0-5f78-a34f-88bd916c25e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186078531 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-27b43ba5-62b8-50b7-a27d-9c99f1e28b1d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-27b43ba5-62b8-50b7-a27d-9c99f1e28b1d" :question/id #uuid "27b43ba5-62b8-50b7-a27d-9c99f1e28b1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-133273" :question/problem "If $x=20\\,\\text{m},$ what is the area of Emma's land plot?\n\nArea $\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we have to evaluate the polynomial ${x}^{2}+3x-10$ at $x=20.$\n\nSo, we substitute $x=20$ into the polynomial and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x-10 & = \\\\ (20)^{2}+3(20)-10 & = \\\\ 400+60-10 & = \\\\ 450\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is $450\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4d2cd95-745f-5a52-99a9-88f26c0bf6c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4d2cd95-745f-5a52-99a9-88f26c0bf6c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d2cd95-745f-5a52-99a9-88f26c0bf6c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2f68f0b0-453f-59b0-bee4-466dbbe05765" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f68f0b0-453f-59b0-bee4-466dbbe05765"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f68f0b0-453f-59b0-bee4-466dbbe05765" :answer/id #uuid "2f68f0b0-453f-59b0-bee4-466dbbe05765" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "450" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068679 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ea9b6670-1349-5317-9261-60b0dc45876e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea9b6670-1349-5317-9261-60b0dc45876e" :question/id #uuid "ea9b6670-1349-5317-9261-60b0dc45876e" :question/math-academy-id "q-133407" :question/problem "![](images/32/327db918054ccc7ac5cb70315023f4714b374994bded724b7451342dfac2d7ee.png)\n\nWhen $x=20\\,\\text{m},$ the dimensions of Emma's plot of land are $25\\,\\text{m}$ and $18\\text{m}.$ A water pipe goes diagonally from one corner to the opposite, as shown above. Find the **exact** length of the pipe.\n\nThe length of the pipe is \n{{field-1}}\nmeters." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we have a right triangle with legs $a=25\\,\\text{m}$ and $b=18\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n![](images/4a/4ac2862067c8e6e868b634003643ef935dbe652e3b46647466dd6b1dc2cb5172.png)\n\nLet $c$ be the length of the hypotenuse. Then, according to the Pythagorean theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}^{2} & ={a}^{2}+{b}^{2} \\\\ c & =\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the values for $a$ and $b$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\sqrt{{25}^{2}+{18}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{625+324} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{949}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4d1f0515-7a1c-51ae-b68f-c8794a460e58"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4d1f0515-7a1c-51ae-b68f-c8794a460e58" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d1f0515-7a1c-51ae-b68f-c8794a460e58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-07c6e8b3-ef8a-59c9-b08b-4995089df7d8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-07c6e8b3-ef8a-59c9-b08b-4995089df7d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07c6e8b3-ef8a-59c9-b08b-4995089df7d8" :answer/id #uuid "07c6e8b3-ef8a-59c9-b08b-4995089df7d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{949}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110626 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-378ec187-f25a-5aab-8757-77a5a8639517"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-378ec187-f25a-5aab-8757-77a5a8639517" :question/id #uuid "378ec187-f25a-5aab-8757-77a5a8639517" :question/math-academy-id "q-133298" :question/problem "Fred purchased his land for $200$ thousand dollars. The value of the land increases by $10%$ each year. How much will Fred's plot be worth after $4$ years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nAfter $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately \n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars." :question/worked-solution "The land initially costs $200$ thousand, which increases at a rate of $10%$ per year.\n\n$10%$ as a decimal is $0.1.$ Therefore, an increase of $10%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1+0.1=1.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n* After $1$ year, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $2$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{2}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $3$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{3}$ thousand dollars.\n\n* After $4$ years, the value will be $200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}$ thousand dollars.\n\nEvaluating the expression for the value after $4$ years, we get the following:\n\n$$\n200\\cdot (1.1)^{4}=292.82≈293,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, after $4$ years, the land will be worth approximately $293$ thousand dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-72f9737f-d640-5da4-81bf-c4cffe514f28"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-72f9737f-d640-5da4-81bf-c4cffe514f28" :answer-field/id #uuid "72f9737f-d640-5da4-81bf-c4cffe514f28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3d4de7d8-5d3b-5a06-9122-9e43959fa9aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3d4de7d8-5d3b-5a06-9122-9e43959fa9aa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d4de7d8-5d3b-5a06-9122-9e43959fa9aa" :answer/id #uuid "3d4de7d8-5d3b-5a06-9122-9e43959fa9aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "293" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147563 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f2953556-ba6d-59b1-a416-ed49b0dde4d7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f2953556-ba6d-59b1-a416-ed49b0dde4d7" :question/id #uuid "f2953556-ba6d-59b1-a416-ed49b0dde4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-133320" :question/problem "The value $V(t)$ of Fred's plot of land, in thousands of dollars, can be modeled by the function $V(t)=200\\cdot (1.1)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in years since he bought the land. What is the growth in the value of the plot over the first two years? Round your answer to the nearest thousand dollars.\n\nFred's land grows in value by\n{{field-1}}\n thousand dollars over the first two years." :question/worked-solution "To find the growth in the value over the first two years, we need to find the value after $2$ years and then subtract the initial cost:\n\n$$\nV(2)-V(0)\n$$\n\nIn the previous part, we were given the initial value of the land:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(0) & =200\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find $V(2),$ the value of the land after $2$ years:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2) & =200\\cdot (1.1)^{(2)} \\\\  & =200\\cdot (1.21) \\\\  & =242\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the first two years is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(2)-V(0) & =242-200 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the growth in the value of the land over the first two years was $42$ thousand dollars." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9349e17e-17ae-52b4-9c0a-f079a5dadf25"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9349e17e-17ae-52b4-9c0a-f079a5dadf25" :answer-field/id #uuid "9349e17e-17ae-52b4-9c0a-f079a5dadf25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f4d23184-fe51-50f2-b7e4-888ce9211c07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4d23184-fe51-50f2-b7e4-888ce9211c07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4d23184-fe51-50f2-b7e4-888ce9211c07" :answer/id #uuid "f4d23184-fe51-50f2-b7e4-888ce9211c07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-aebdb39b-39ee-5d00-9cb7-4aa2d3d454f5" :multistep/id #uuid "aebdb39b-39ee-5d00-9cb7-4aa2d3d454f5" :multistep/context "![](images/ae/ae53eea7c3f8aa948d734ca8bb70d6d555637b5d3a0048d6787e2a75dbadfb98.png)\n\nFred owns a rectangular plot of land, as shown above. The area of the plot is $2{x}^{2}+6x$ square meters, and its width is $x+3$ meters, where $x>10$ is a fixed constant." :multistep/steps ["step-3db731f4-5240-5811-93c7-3394a14bf49b" "step-43459601-8ced-51fb-b2f5-0490414b2238" "step-82450113-651d-5d2b-bc4c-b0e