nswer-fields ["answer-field-fb4215bc-72a0-5b26-849f-c9beda735571"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb4215bc-72a0-5b26-849f-c9beda735571" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb4215bc-72a0-5b26-849f-c9beda735571" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fca98fd5-ad67-577b-8e53-b21a5d00cfce" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fca98fd5-ad67-577b-8e53-b21a5d00cfce" "answer-b1726c6b-9323-5020-8612-d6d390d86ec3" "answer-a2638610-e6c2-5b19-a062-611e48b7e4d6" "answer-064cbb47-f269-513e-9de2-2b990bb30623" "answer-d8208668-f192-5063-946d-65284aaf0d5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fca98fd5-ad67-577b-8e53-b21a5d00cfce" :answer/id #uuid "fca98fd5-ad67-577b-8e53-b21a5d00cfce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1726c6b-9323-5020-8612-d6d390d86ec3" :answer/id #uuid "b1726c6b-9323-5020-8612-d6d390d86ec3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2638610-e6c2-5b19-a062-611e48b7e4d6" :answer/id #uuid "a2638610-e6c2-5b19-a062-611e48b7e4d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-064cbb47-f269-513e-9de2-2b990bb30623" :answer/id #uuid "064cbb47-f269-513e-9de2-2b990bb30623" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8208668-f192-5063-946d-65284aaf0d5f" :answer/id #uuid "d8208668-f192-5063-946d-65284aaf0d5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272022 :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Find the orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The orthogonal projection of $a$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 8+1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 10+2\\cdot 6}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{8+4-10+12}{1+1+1+4}b \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134233 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 12+6\\cdot 6+(-6)\\cdot (-6)+2\\cdot 12}{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{24+36+36+24}{4+36+36+4}a \\\\  & =\\frac{120}{80}a \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}a \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186326839 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find the distance between $x$ and $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The distance between the vector $x$ and the vector space $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}$ is given by $‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖$, where $\\overset{^}{x}$ in the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{span}\\{b\\}.$\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{b\\cdot x}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 3}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\text{ }\\,b \\\\  & =\\frac{6+0+6+0}{9+0+9+0}\\text{ }\\,b \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\text{ }\\,b \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+((-4)-0)^{2}+((-2)-(-2))^{2}+(3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{0+16+0+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186398431 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-5)+6\\cdot 5+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{-10+30+6+4}{4+36+4+16}a \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{60}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186272024 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Find the distance between $x$ and $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The distance between the vector $x$ and the vector space $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}$ is given by $‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖$, where $\\overset{^}{x}$ in the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{span}\\{b\\}.$\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{b\\}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{b\\cdot x}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot (-2)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{6+4+2}{4+1+1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\overset{^}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖x-\\overset{^}{x}‖ & =\\sqrt{({x}_{1}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{1})^{2}+({x}_{2}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{2})^{2}+({x}_{3}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{3})^{2}+({x}_{4}-{\\overset{^}{x}}_{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-4)^{2}+(4-2)^{2}+(-2-(-2))^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+0+4} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354506 :question/problem "Let $x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ If $x=y+z,$ where $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ then $z=$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d8/d887852fe51f08cb7296437e69b6a9632721cfd155558e6abf9a99b4f59f7ad6.png)\n\nFirst, we find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto $\\text{Span}\\{a\\}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}a \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 4}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}}a \\\\  & =\\frac{3+2+(-2)+12}{1+4+1+9}a \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{15}a \\\\  & =a \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ z & =x-{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $y\\in \\text{Span}\\{a\\}$ and $z⊥\\text{Span}\\{a\\},$ as shown in the picture above.\n\nIn conclusion, $z=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-b5c59f3b-4545-527f-bcce-f690660ef3e4" :question/id #uuid "b5c59f3b-4545-527f-bcce-f690660ef3e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81194" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{5}b \\\\  & =2b \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a8a9e1ef-32a3-5061-963d-fc7b1deca773"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a8a9e1ef-32a3-5061-963d-fc7b1deca773" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8a9e1ef-32a3-5061-963d-fc7b1deca773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7906b069-985b-55da-bffc-9c6e27d07cf1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5a31f33c-d9ce-5796-96ff-2ecd20ff3a04" "answer-1b0c7c11-5bc7-5182-ae49-3f48dbd7c9b3" "answer-4813ecdc-f225-5302-97c1-42d0f5b7ebff" "answer-ebebfc1d-adfa-58b5-9818-67b07f067015" "answer-7906b069-985b-55da-bffc-9c6e27d07cf1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a31f33c-d9ce-5796-96ff-2ecd20ff3a04" :answer/id #uuid "5a31f33c-d9ce-5796-96ff-2ecd20ff3a04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b0c7c11-5bc7-5182-ae49-3f48dbd7c9b3" :answer/id #uuid "1b0c7c11-5bc7-5182-ae49-3f48dbd7c9b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4813ecdc-f225-5302-97c1-42d0f5b7ebff" :answer/id #uuid "4813ecdc-f225-5302-97c1-42d0f5b7ebff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebebfc1d-adfa-58b5-9818-67b07f067015" :answer/id #uuid "ebebfc1d-adfa-58b5-9818-67b07f067015" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7906b069-985b-55da-bffc-9c6e27d07cf1" :answer/id #uuid "7906b069-985b-55da-bffc-9c6e27d07cf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351257 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of  linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{10}b \\\\  & =b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-4cd83f2a-cc48-509b-8020-7c5ca2609e27" :question/id #uuid "4cd83f2a-cc48-509b-8020-7c5ca2609e27" :question/math-academy-id "q-86745" :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-4)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-6\\,}{\\,\\,2\\,}b \\\\  & =-3b \\\\  & =-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f7164e41-9f76-56e4-a6df-6aa236623be7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f7164e41-9f76-56e4-a6df-6aa236623be7" :answer-field/id #uuid "f7164e41-9f76-56e4-a6df-6aa236623be7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2cb154ad-9683-5421-88f3-197c44a79efa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2cb154ad-9683-5421-88f3-197c44a79efa" "answer-f474dbed-7a32-51fb-8452-53b1feb62da5" "answer-1b2c294d-15d5-5493-91d3-b1f32cb60399" "answer-0b9d59f2-2420-5ba9-8f84-fd903cd8f9ed" "answer-599af1e9-81b4-5515-805c-1c879ef814a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2cb154ad-9683-5421-88f3-197c44a79efa" :answer/id #uuid "2cb154ad-9683-5421-88f3-197c44a79efa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f474dbed-7a32-51fb-8452-53b1feb62da5" :answer/id #uuid "f474dbed-7a32-51fb-8452-53b1feb62da5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b2c294d-15d5-5493-91d3-b1f32cb60399" :answer/id #uuid "1b2c294d-15d5-5493-91d3-b1f32cb60399" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b9d59f2-2420-5ba9-8f84-fd903cd8f9ed" :answer/id #uuid "0b9d59f2-2420-5ba9-8f84-fd903cd8f9ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-599af1e9-81b4-5515-805c-1c879ef814a4" :answer/id #uuid "599af1e9-81b4-5515-805c-1c879ef814a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134234 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ -15 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-2(\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3})=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-10)\\cdot 6+(-15)\\cdot 3+28\\cdot 5}{{6}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{-60-45+140}{36+9+25}b \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{70}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186394133 :question/problem "Given the vector $x$ and the system of linear equations below, find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system.\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find a basis of the solution space of the system. \n\nConsider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system, which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are $2$ pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is a basis of the solution space of the system.\n\nFinally, we find the projection of $x$ onto the solution space of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,x & =\\frac{x\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (-1)+3\\cdot 1+7\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{3}b \\\\  & =4b \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135046 :tutorial/content "The **orthogonal projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto a span $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ is the vector in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ that is \"closest\" to $\\mathbf{x}.$ Let's work an example to clarify what we mean by \"closest.\"\n\nConsider the following two vectors:\n\n![](images/00/0079b00022e2f536fc3cc60db20657be10024ed81bcca3f802d2b25d8072c0ce.svg) \n\nGeometrically, $\\mathbf{x}$ can be written as the sum of a vector $k{\\mathbf{a}}$ that lies in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ and \nanother vector $\\mathbf{z}$ that belongs to the orthogonal complement of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$, as illustrated in the picture below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4754bf048aa6c05cd7ef574af306150c30d435f90afec36f492ed4b8013e899.png)\n\nThe vector $k\\mathbf a$ is called the **orthogonal projection** of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as the sum\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = k \\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{z},\n$$\n\nfor some $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and ${\\mathbf{z}} \\perp \\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nTo compute the orthogonal projection $k \\mathbf{a},$ we can first find the dot product of both sides of the equation above with $\\mathbf{a}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot a & =(ka+z)\\cdot a \\\\ x\\cdot a & =k(a\\cdot a)+z\\cdot a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{z},$ we have $\\mathbf{z} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = 0.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot a & =k(a\\cdot a) \\\\ k & =\\frac{x\\cdot a}{a\\cdot a} \\\\ k & =\\frac{5\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-6)+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 6}{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the orthogonal projection is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** There is a shortcut! Instead of going through this process each time, we can compute the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}\\}$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{a}\\,x & =\\frac{a\\cdot x}{a\\cdot a}\\,a\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134001 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186052076 :answer-field/choices 17592186052080] [:db/retract 17592186052076 :answer-field/choices 17592186052081] [:db/retract 17592186052076 :answer-field/choices 17592186052077] [:db/retract 17592186052076 :answer-field/choices 17592186052078] [:db/retract 17592186052076 :answer-field/choices 17592186052079] {:db/id 17592186054704 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2158d6ec-2be1-5446-9223-07b6831e7100" "question-24348bb2-c07f-5da2-b2dd-e3e224260e04"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054351 :question/problem "Simplify $a^{9}\\cdot a^{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}^{9}\\cdot {a}^{7} & = \\\\ {a}^{9+7} & = \\\\ {a}^{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054705 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7907920d-205f-5377-afe5-d6463114236a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054352 :question/worked-solution "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{2+3+4} & = \\\\ {x}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore $x^{n}=x^9$, so $n=9.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054706 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e6c10cd-7db6-5a24-8c7d-80ae10691af2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054353 :question/problem "Simplify $x\\left(x^3 y^5\\right) \\left(x^2 y^9\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "Here we have two variables, $x$ and $y.$ We will start by grouping the two variables separately: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({x}^{3}{y}^{5})({x}^{2}{y}^{9}) & = \\\\ {x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {y}^{5}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\cdot {y}^{9} & = \\\\ ({x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})({y}^{5}\\cdot {y}^{9})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{2})({y}^{5}\\cdot {y}^{9}) & = \\\\ {x}^{(1+3+2)}\\cdot {y}^{(5+9)} & = \\\\ {x}^{6}{y}^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-2158d6ec-2be1-5446-9223-07b6831e7100" :question/id #uuid "2158d6ec-2be1-5446-9223-07b6831e7100" :question/math-academy-id "q-388" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $x\\cdot {x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We add the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{1}\\cdot {x}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{1+2} & = \\\\ {x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a8328799-896e-5212-8c80-2907af8b6d2a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a8328799-896e-5212-8c80-2907af8b6d2a" :answer-field/id #uuid "a8328799-896e-5212-8c80-2907af8b6d2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c638a612-ac6b-5641-b143-681bed9091a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c5e9a546-66e7-5266-9000-f8a352e3b453" "answer-52df8e4e-e473-5190-9aeb-f0096927f5fb" "answer-c638a612-ac6b-5641-b143-681bed9091a3" "answer-97487b45-8238-58f3-9c27-00ee8e3855c1" "answer-d0395dd1-17ba-540c-87b8-c76d002accac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5e9a546-66e7-5266-9000-f8a352e3b453" :answer/id #uuid "c5e9a546-66e7-5266-9000-f8a352e3b453" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52df8e4e-e473-5190-9aeb-f0096927f5fb" :answer/id #uuid "52df8e4e-e473-5190-9aeb-f0096927f5fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{-3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c638a612-ac6b-5641-b143-681bed9091a3" :answer/id #uuid "c638a612-ac6b-5641-b143-681bed9091a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97487b45-8238-58f3-9c27-00ee8e385