5b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-95450425-9751-51d9-bdf4-48d8be8b762f" "answer-1ca88955-2551-56b5-ae77-f58ffc8d46ca" "answer-20a27ccd-ef69-5159-a07a-6bb960f42037" "answer-23e3fcda-86fd-5c67-80ac-0a18aa283291" "answer-ce847297-eafd-5ed8-a2c0-a653698ee15b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95450425-9751-51d9-bdf4-48d8be8b762f" :answer/id #uuid "95450425-9751-51d9-bdf4-48d8be8b762f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ca88955-2551-56b5-ae77-f58ffc8d46ca" :answer/id #uuid "1ca88955-2551-56b5-ae77-f58ffc8d46ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20a27ccd-ef69-5159-a07a-6bb960f42037" :answer/id #uuid "20a27ccd-ef69-5159-a07a-6bb960f42037" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{3}\\cdot 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23e3fcda-86fd-5c67-80ac-0a18aa283291" :answer/id #uuid "23e3fcda-86fd-5c67-80ac-0a18aa283291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce847297-eafd-5ed8-a2c0-a653698ee15b" :answer/id #uuid "ce847297-eafd-5ed8-a2c0-a653698ee15b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{2}^{3}\\cdot {3}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055118 :question/problem "Use exponents to rewrite the product $b\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}\\cdot {b}^{7}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here we have two variables, $b$ and $c.$ We will start by grouping the two variables separately: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}\\cdot {b}^{7} & = \\\\ {b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7}\\cdot {c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6} & = \\\\ ({b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7})\\cdot ({c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis way, we can apply the product rule to the two variables independently:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({b}^{1}\\cdot {b}^{2}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot {b}^{7})\\cdot ({c}^{3}\\cdot {c}^{5}\\cdot {c}^{6}) & = \\\\ {b}^{(1+2+4+7)}\\cdot {c}^{(3+5+6)} & = \\\\ {b}^{14}{c}^{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186052076 :answer-field/correct "answer-ee7d415e-0961-5b0a-98e7-925e1bdf7a10" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ee7d415e-0961-5b0a-98e7-925e1bdf7a10" "answer-026f402f-4f08-5e98-9638-1f92ff4899a6" "answer-1a3209b9-00a2-5a1a-a7a0-639a1f4a7573" "answer-f0e41fa0-f099-5121-8725-02fe13cbd7b8" "answer-81252667-4f78-5e9b-ba67-141d2bb31903"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee7d415e-0961-5b0a-98e7-925e1bdf7a10" :answer/id #uuid "ee7d415e-0961-5b0a-98e7-925e1bdf7a10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{b}^{14}\\cdot {c}^{14}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-026f402f-4f08-5e98-9638-1f92ff4899a6" :answer/id #uuid "026f402f-4f08-5e98-9638-1f92ff4899a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{b}^{18}\\cdot {c}^{11}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a3209b9-00a2-5a1a-a7a0-639a1f4a7573" :answer/id #uuid "1a3209b9-00a2-5a1a-a7a0-639a1f4a7573" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{b}^{4}\\cdot {c}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0e41fa0-f099-5121-8725-02fe13cbd7b8" :answer/id #uuid "f0e41fa0-f099-5121-8725-02fe13cbd7b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{b}^{6}\\cdot {c}^{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81252667-4f78-5e9b-ba67-141d2bb31903" :answer/id #uuid "81252667-4f78-5e9b-ba67-141d2bb31903" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{b}^{7}\\cdot {c}^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186055948 :tutorial/content "The **product rule for exponents** states that when we multiply two or more exponents that have the same base, we add the exponents:\n\n$$\na^n\\cdot a^m=a^{n+m}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\na^2\\cdot a^3 = a^{2+3} = a^5.\n$$\n\nTo see why the product rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two factors $a^2$ and $a^3$ we have $a^2=a\\cdot a$ and $a^3= a\\cdot a\\cdot a.$ If we now substitute these two expressions into $a^2\\cdot a^3$, we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\na^2\\cdot a^3&=\\\n (a\\cdot a) \\cdot (a\\cdot a\\cdot a)&=\\\na\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a\\cdot a&=\\\na^5\n}\n$$\n\nThe product rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054921 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186127687 :answer-field/choices 17592186127688] [:db/retract 17592186127687 :answer-field/choices 17592186127689] [:db/retract 17592186127687 :answer-field/choices 17592186127690] [:db/retract 17592186127687 :answer-field/choices 17592186127691] [:db/retract 17592186127687 :answer-field/choices 17592186127692] {:db/id 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-18c92889-12f7-533b-bf2f-74218efc0204"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129032 :question/problem "Let $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C},$ respectively. If $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $\\mathcal{C}$ to basis $\\mathcal{B},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}$\n\nII. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$\n\nIII. $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129130 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-090d811d-2518-5f77-a8e6-7fb33e3ee0bb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128941 :question/problem "![](images/32/32b9b4bef130167d1da59e89933dea01a699ca4308decf54403f62ffb582ec20.svg)\n\nConsider the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129131 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-79dda897-ef6c-5dc8-918f-90eef254a83f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128942 :question/problem "![](images/6d/6dea9ab921904f09ef76230fe87194b745f4f06eb9cfbbbbda0b3bd44722fc93.svg)\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{S}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n![](images/01/01d05455345f8ec71e58f613d3bcf54c41f4ff196766c728871e54050f590fba.svg)\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-7)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 13 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129155 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d84dee64-5460-51d7-b92e-0ebbf9e09851"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128973 :question/problem "![](images/4e/4ef9218607e5b3285d98967ec7cf44627ece6ecc5918f702fded329b1fe8ee4d.svg)\n\nConsider the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n![](images/26/26210267b80b50c4296225ac67237767cb326f2863fbfeba4cbdeb3f256ef62e.svg)\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n![](images/97/97450c835af63c4489b848a69986d5b496af1bf983355c65021aca3384826ca7.svg)\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-18c92889-12f7-533b-bf2f-74218efc0204" :question/id #uuid "18c92889-12f7-533b-bf2f-74218efc0204" :question/math-academy-id "q-38361" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $[T]_{B}=[T]_{C}^{-1}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}$\n\nII. $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$\n\nIII. $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2e/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd483cd6-8476-5430-add9-76a6040fadd1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd483cd6-8476-5430-add9-76a6040fadd1" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd483cd6-8476-5430-add9-76a6040fadd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7a019de8-fdbb-5874-b9a3-954a21bd5b5c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f6f097ce-9807-535f-aa3b-32eb24813105" "answer-a361a1e4-9e64-5e97-880b-6eacdecce1af" "answer-7532fabe-190b-58ef-a25b-c7b41103ed86" "answer-0318a7cd-6ed8-5929-a663-a34a94cdf12b" "answer-7a019de8-fdbb-5874-b9a3-954a21bd5b5c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6f097ce-9807-535f-aa3b-32eb24813105" :answer/id #uuid "f6f097ce-9807-535f-aa3b-32eb24813105" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a361a1e4-9e64-5e97-880b-6eacdecce1af" :answer/id #uuid "a361a1e4-9e64-5e97-880b-6eacdecce1af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7532fabe-190b-58ef-a25b-c7b41103ed86" :answer/id #uuid "7532fabe-190b-58ef-a25b-c7b41103ed86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0318a7cd-6ed8-5929-a663-a34a94cdf12b" :answer/id #uuid "0318a7cd-6ed8-5929-a663-a34a94cdf12b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a019de8-fdbb-5874-b9a3-954a21bd5b5c" :answer/id #uuid "7a019de8-fdbb-5874-b9a3-954a21bd5b5c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-090d811d-2518-5f77-a8e6-7fb33e3ee0bb" :question/id #uuid "090d811d-2518-5f77-a8e6-7fb33e3ee0bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-97113" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 \\\\ -14 & 11\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4bb5b3db-09a2-5068-870b-5e729655be69"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4bb5b3db-09a2-5068-870b-5e729655be69" :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb5b3db-09a2-5068-870b-5e729655be69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-477ace4c-82e3-57c5-a4c3-6fc1dcc1600b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-477ace4c-82e3-57c5-a4c3-6fc1dcc1600b" "answer-c9be896a-c20c-55d4-9452-71c1e8bfd917" "answer-e57c7b8f-280a-5e63-a865-80fca1154fb6" "answer-5fa983bc-fb00-52bc-b27d-c56b5262d607" "answer-6888f16e-6a85-521f-975e-63342031a0b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-477ace4c-82e3-57c5-a4c3-6fc1dcc1600b" :answer/id #uuid "477ace4c-82e3-57c5-a4c3-6fc1dcc1600b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9be896a-c20c-55d4-9452-71c1e8bfd917" :answer/id #uuid "c9be896a-c20c-55d4-9452-71c1e8bfd917" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 7 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e57c7b8f-280a-5e63-a865-80fca1154fb6" :answer/id #uuid "e57c7b8f-280a-5e63-a865-80fca1154fb6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fa983bc-fb00-52bc-b27d-c56b5262d607" :answer/id #uuid "5fa983bc-fb00-52bc-b27d-c56b5262d607" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -10 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6888f16e-6a85-521f-975e-63342031a0b7" :answer/id #uuid "6888f16e-6a85-521f-975e-63342031a0b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186419684 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186395243 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129641 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-79dda897-ef6c-5dc8-918f-90eef254a83f" :question/id #uuid "79dda897-ef6c-5dc8-918f-90eef254a83f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97132" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/db/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 7}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 41 \\\\ 5 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & -11 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-86fb28cc-fb24-5ba3-a4c6-598034db31b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-86fb28cc-fb24-5ba3-a4c6-598034db31b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "86fb28cc-fb24-5ba3-a4c6-598034db31b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-25d0c1b4-61e7-5e25-9833-9ff8521f8e61" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b1a541f3-2fce-5116-8f9a-bbdea23325fc" "answer-a84373ce-6910-52c1-988d-e8fbee200996" "answer-c5c07fb2-8a16-5c6a-8258-d32151d0964c" "answer-f7563451-68a5-5c50-b602-20f48ffbfffe" "answer-25d0c1b4-61e7-5e25-9833-9ff8521f8e61"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b1a541f3-2fce-5116-8f9a-bbdea23325fc" :answer/id #uuid "b1a541f3-2fce-5116-8f9a-bbdea23325fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 15 \\\\ 3 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a84373ce-6910-52c1-988d-e8fbee200996" :answer/id #uuid "a84373ce-6910-52c1-988d-e8fbee200996" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5c07fb2-8a16-5c6a-8258-d32151d0964c" :answer/id #uuid "c5c07fb2-8a16-5c6a-8258-d32151d0964c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7563451-68a5-5c50-b602-20f48ffbfffe" :answer/id #uuid "f7563451-68a5-5c50-b602-20f48ffbfffe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25d0c1b4-61e7-5e25-9833-9ff8521f8e61" :answer/id #uuid "25d0c1b4-61e7-5e25-9833-9ff8521f8e61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -11 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360755 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360776 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129701 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 7-5\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-13 & 9 \\\\ -16 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186127687 :answer-field/correct "answer-7146fe46-6a51-56c2-a398-39cdace60f8f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7146fe46-6a51-56c2-a398-39cdace60f8f" "answer-4ab2b0d1-3d5f-53f9-8f37-02b8cfded0cb" "answer-1079d3d2-be2f-5cb3-b94c-9018f0762790" "answer-53b397b9-c829-5c9b-b0ea-a550e68d9a00" "answer-702184cc-df91-5774-bfc1-66a9113e145d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7146fe46-6a51-56c2-a398-39cdace60f8f" :answer/id #uuid "7146fe46-6a51-56c2-a398-39cdace60f8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-13 & 9 \\\\ -16 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ab2b0d1-3d5f-53f9-8f37-02b8cfded0cb" :answer/id #uuid "4ab2b0d1-3d5f-53f9-8f37-02b8cfded0cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -8 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1079d3d2-be2f-5cb3-b94c-9018f0762790" :answer/id #uuid "1079d3d2-be2f-5cb3-b94c-9018f0762790" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}15 & -38 \\\\ 8 & -19\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53b397b9-c829-5c9b-b0ea-a550e68d9a00" :answer/id #uuid "53b397b9-c829-5c9b-b0ea-a550e68d9a00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & -8 \\\\ 38 & -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-702184cc-df91-5774-bfc1-66a9113e145d" :answer/id #uuid "702184cc-df91-5774-bfc1-66a9113e145d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}19 & 19 \\\\ 38 & 57\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186419685 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -12 \\\\ 7 & 14\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d84dee64-5460-51d7-b92e-0ebbf9e09851" :question/id #uuid "d84dee64-5460-51d7-b92e-0ebbf9e09851" :question/math-academy-id "q-140486" :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & -9 \\\\ 10 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -11 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8dc1988-bf2a-5231-bb4f-7e3b1ddd7888"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8dc1988-bf2a-5231-bb4f-7e3b1ddd7888" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8dc1988-bf2a-5231-bb4f-7e3b1ddd7888" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22b7fe3f-06c5-50bf-974c-0d0810764de8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-22b7fe3f-06c5-50bf-974c-0d0810764de8" "answer-2861eb9c-4514-5616-ba03-e8561c891eed" "answer-4dcfa639-1ecc-55b1-9be1-e5cb4a37903f" "answer-18d7f229-8e52-50a6-aefb-b0cac4224645" "answer-50c0d953-a401-5471-947d-1ca57c74ec8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22b7fe3f-06c5-50bf-974c-0d0810764de8" :answer/id #uuid "22b7fe3f-06c5-50bf-974c-0d0810764de8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -11 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2861eb9c-4514-5616-ba03-e8561c891eed" :answer/id #uuid "2861eb9c-4514-5616-ba03-e8561c891eed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -5 \\\\ 11 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dcfa639-1ecc-55b1-9be1-e5cb4a37903f" :answer/id #uuid "4dcfa639-1ecc-55b1-9be1-e5cb4a37903f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -10 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18d7f229-8e52-50a6-aefb-b0cac4224645" :answer/id #uuid "18d7f229-8e52-50a6-aefb-b0cac4224645" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -11 \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50c0d953-a401-5471-947d-1ca57c74ec8b" :answer/id #uuid "50c0d953-a401-5471-947d-1ca57c74ec8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 \\\\ 10 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360777 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -7 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130348 :tutorial/content "Recall that the matrices $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T} : V \\to V$ relative to two bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ of a vector space $V$ are different.\n\nIf we are given the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ how do we find the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}?$\nTo find $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}},$ we use the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nThe diagram below is a commutative diagram that shows how a change of basis works at each step.\n\n![](images/df/dff02d9f46eb7ded23f418765a50dd1971145cd0e585a82beeef1a45812df8a7.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. So algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor instance, if ![](images/89/89530292f17bb402acb489d90a17e7628a33bab37165b02580479b116e6a2b23.svg) and ![](images/f7/f75100cf6de9ccdb537d685f4e2858278214bbe351c9828e8a94a58628c1151b.svg), we use this formula to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the formula for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Alternatively, if we know the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\to {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}$ from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B},$ we can construct a similar diagram:\n\n![](images/d6/d6cd195ccd42804ea91debabd23563bb0f6903fdd1b5d708b712c75417fff2a9.png)\n\nFrom this, we can derive the equation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} =\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130349 :tutorial/content "Consider the bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\, \\ldots, \\, \\mathbf{b}_n \\}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\, \\ldots, \\, \\mathbf{c}_n \\}$ of a vector space $V,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}.$\nThen the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}} =P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small {\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}} \\to {\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change of basis matrix from ${\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}$ to ${\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nWhen written in expanded form, this formula becomes:\n\n![](images/0c/0c782638ef4254884c9521b8c36452d241d4e95acb5f4c54ecd31b9045490143.svg)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129357 :activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-750b7607-258b-55b2-8f3f-8eb360c8a574"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-750b7607-258b-55b2-8f3f-8eb360c8a574" :question/id #uuid "750b7607-258b-55b2-8f3f-8eb360c8a574" :question/math-academy-id "q-94644" :question/problem "Let $V$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in Q.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $V.$\n\nII. For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in V,$ we have $p(x)+0=p(x).$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in V,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as\n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $0\\in V.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in V,$ we have\n\n$$\np(x)+0=p(x)\n$$\n\nsince $0$ is the unique zero element in the set of all polynomials.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(t),q(t)\\in V,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(t)+q(t)=q(t)+p(t).\n$$\n\nHence, the addition of polynomials in $V$ is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1ca2ea90-b950-5f61-9fa7-e9762d8343dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1ca2ea90-b950-5f61-9fa7-e9762d8343dd" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ca2ea90-b950-5f61-9fa7-e9762d8343dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f6333884-91ca-5deb-b697-754d8fa83902" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83d03ad1-699f-5271-aa16-12a88da19753" "answer-a6e264d4-7eff-58c9-af68-052f1a966bca" "answer-712e9086-50d5-5be4-8bd2-838841d9d6bd" "answer-f6333884-91ca-5deb-b697-754d8fa83902" "answer-48152817-d761-520e-9162-c1b4b1f93594"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83d03ad1-699f-5271-aa16-12a88da19753" :answer/id #uuid "83d03ad1-699f-5271-aa16-12a88da19753" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6e264d4-7eff-58c9-af68-052f1a966bca" :answer/id #uuid "a6e264d4-7eff-58c9-af68-052f1a966bca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-712e9086-50d5-5be4-8bd2-838841d9d6bd" :answer/id #uuid "712e9086-50d5-5be4-8bd2-838841d9d6bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6333884-91ca-5deb-b697-754d8fa83902" :answer/id #uuid "f6333884-91ca-5deb-b697-754d8fa83902" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48152817-d761-520e-9162-c1b4b1f93594" :answer/id #uuid "48152817-d761-520e-9162-c1b4b1f93594" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166926 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-da4c8dfe-99e9-5427-8a0d-f6ac8ce055dd" "question-711de219-9297-5bb8-aae2-ee7607fb16cd" "question-6036bb50-cd4e-590c-9c42-05fdc24940b7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166561 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=6x(1-0.1x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-7y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x) - 3xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $6x(1-0.1x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-3xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y = 0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\nx' = 6x(1-0.1x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x(1-0.1x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $10.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6f820e7d-e65e-5149-80bc-fca1cd9160a9" "question-f57e9bc6-8d1b-532c-906d-19c1ec051963"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166558 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-0.2x)-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the equilibria of the model." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x(1-0.2x)-4xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x(1-0.2x)-4x(0) & =0 \\\\ 5x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2)(1-0.2(2))-(4)(2)y & =0 \\\\ 10(1-0.4)-8y & =0 \\\\ 10(0.6)-8y & =0 \\\\ 6-8y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2, 0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n(5, 0), \\qquad\n(2, 0.75).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166928 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bee04d0f-21c8-5845-b5c1-0985dbd842b5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166559 :question/problem "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 2.5]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=5x(1-x)-xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x(1-x)-xy)=5-10x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+2xy)=-1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/52/52a848e561f814865aa10f5196bdf756a7f3a83a65e2cb5442f6df17709f2a1d.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/cb/cbdbf12c92fcce1f2fedcf11402643b8bbe75bf08b48de3f0d36a0a0bb814d2d.svg) to get\n\n![](images/29/29b4d10f386df1ad331ef73a88986089cf90d566128ee8abc69dcad53392ed3b.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2.5-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 5 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2.5-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-5(-0.5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (2.5+\\lambda )+2.5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2.5\\lambda +2.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1,2} = -1.25 \\pm 0.25\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable spiral sink." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166929 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-bcc29b37-0226-5337-b5dc-f223113388b1" "question-2da83aef-8248-5784-87a2-78e988b82e95"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166560 :question/problem "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 0]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-3xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-5y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1-2x)-3xy)=4-16x-3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1-2x)-3xy)=-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y+4xy)=-5+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/ef/ef96765bdead0580d208e3a7303b9f74443a1154df877b6f00b085c7a2a60f07.svg)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/62/6282a23eb5bd51c399548652efe32b63b2b69b661776c99b2aa2168a2d11366c.svg) to get\n\n![](images/90/902cae135690e7001927c07cfd15e72805cb65d7ba645659e0c30f19e2016226.svg)\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -4$ and $\\lambda_2=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable sink." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370383 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x)-1.6xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2.4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x)-1.6xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $3.2x(1-0.05x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-1.6xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.2x(1-0.05x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.2x(1-0.05x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=20.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $20.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370389 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-3xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $2x(1-0.2x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-3xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=5.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-da4c8dfe-99e9-5427-8a0d-f6ac8ce055dd" :question/id #uuid "da4c8dfe-99e9-5427-8a0d-f6ac8ce055dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335573" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x)-2.2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-7y+2.8xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x)-2.2xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $3.6x(1-0.02x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-2.2xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=3.6x(1-0.02x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.6x(1-0.02x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=50.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $50.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c17b32bc-6591-55dc-9066-69fac3398247"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c17b32bc-6591-55dc-9066-69fac3398247" :answer-field/id #uuid "c17b32bc-6591-55dc-9066-69fac3398247" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ed44adc7-5385-5f62-b484-b63621da0437" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed44adc7-5385-5f62-b484-b63621da0437"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed44adc7-5385-5f62-b484-b63621da0437" :answer/id #uuid "ed44adc7-5385-5f62-b484-b63621da0437" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-711de219-9297-5bb8-aae2-ee7607fb16cd" :question/id #uuid "711de219-9297-5bb8-aae2-ee7607fb16cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-335565" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=1.8x(1-0.1x)-0.7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4.5y+1.1xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.8x(1-0.1x)-0.7xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $1.8x(1-0.1x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-0.7xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.8x(1-0.1x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.8x(1-0.1x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=10.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6f6fefb-5bbc-5ec7-ae71-6e83483bfa39"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6f6fefb-5bbc-5ec7-ae71-6e83483bfa39" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6f6fefb-5bbc-5ec7-ae71-6e83483bfa39" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-13cd005f-cc1e-5418-b01a-3d30eaeacf25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-13cd005f-cc1e-5418-b01a-3d30eaeacf25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13cd005f-cc1e-5418-b01a-3d30eaeacf25" :answer/id #uuid "13cd005f-cc1e-5418-b01a-3d30eaeacf25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6036bb50-cd4e-590c-9c42-05fdc24940b7" :question/id #uuid "6036bb50-cd4e-590c-9c42-05fdc24940b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335563" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x)-0.8xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+1.2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x)-0.8xy\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $1.5x(1-0.5x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-0.8xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=1.5x(1-0.5x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5x(1-0.5x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f1969079-3801-5540-ad52-a2686eb7dcd2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f1969079-3801-5540-ad52-a2686eb7dcd2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1969079-3801-5540-ad52-a2686eb7dcd2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-80867b85-3d8a-55aa-805d-f126483fe5fc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80867b85-3d8a-55aa-805d-f126483fe5fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80867b85-3d8a-55aa-805d-f126483fe5fc" :answer/id #uuid "80867b85-3d8a-55aa-805d-f126483fe5fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370402 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.25x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.25x)-xy=0 \\\\ -2y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.25x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.25(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(2,1.5).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6f820e7d-e65e-5149-80bc-fca1cd9160a9" :question/id #uuid "6f820e7d-e65e-5149-80bc-fca1cd9160a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335587" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.2x)-xy=0 \\\\ -2y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-2+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.2x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.2(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 3.6-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.8).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(5,0),\\,(2,1.8).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b0e2165-d2ff-5713-97bf-c955e40a3141" "answer-field-d6eed6df-13ab-5453-bace-370fffcaabbf" "answer-field-2ba92e4a-e5e2-517a-a37c-5e3d9f7d7c95"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b0e2165-d2ff-5713-97bf-c955e40a3141" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b0e2165-d2ff-5713-97bf-c955e40a3141" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a293993b-6ac4-540e-b8d9-0b69ee5d0bee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a293993b-6ac4-540e-b8d9-0b69ee5d0bee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a293993b-6ac4-540e-b8d9-0b69ee5d0bee" :answer/id #uuid "a293993b-6ac4-540e-b8d9-0b69ee5d0bee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6eed6df-13ab-5453-bace-370fffcaabbf" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6eed6df-13ab-5453-bace-370fffcaabbf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ea1c97dc-8a48-557d-8ba1-1550712b426f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea1c97dc-8a48-557d-8ba1-1550712b426f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea1c97dc-8a48-557d-8ba1-1550712b426f" :answer/id #uuid "ea1c97dc-8a48-557d-8ba1-1550712b426f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ba92e4a-e5e2-517a-a37c-5e3d9f7d7c95" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba92e4a-e5e2-517a-a37c-5e3d9f7d7c95" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8ba4cc46-2fcc-57e3-81c8-3fc4c3d92f5b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ba4cc46-2fcc-57e3-81c8-3fc4c3d92f5b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ba4cc46-2fcc-57e3-81c8-3fc4c3d92f5b" :answer/id #uuid "8ba4cc46-2fcc-57e3-81c8-3fc4c3d92f5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f57e9bc6-8d1b-532c-906d-19c1ec051963" :question/id #uuid "f57e9bc6-8d1b-532c-906d-19c1ec051963" :question/math-academy-id "q-335608" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x(1-0.1x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.1x)-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x(1-0.1x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 3x(0.1x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(10,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)(1-0.1(2))-2(2)y & =0 \\\\ 4.8-4y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.2).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(10,0),\\,(2,1.2).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f58dfdf0-1299-5477-82f2-6f8e9cc6f416" "answer-field-46f12926-e806-5294-840b-8aafdee3fa78" "answer-field-7b40650d-078e-5ff0-ae71-e108848a91a7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f58dfdf0-1299-5477-82f2-6f8e9cc6f416" :answer-field/id #uuid "f58dfdf0-1299-5477-82f2-6f8e9cc6f416" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e30b980f-3d8b-5694-9e98-0bb533c42acc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e30b980f-3d8b-5694-9e98-0bb533c42acc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e30b980f-3d8b-5694-9e98-0bb533c42acc" :answer/id #uuid "e30b980f-3d8b-5694-9e98-0bb533c42acc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46f12926-e806-5294-840b-8aafdee3fa78" :answer-field/id #uuid "46f12926-e806-5294-840b-8aafdee3fa78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bfc85a5f-9ea0-5c99-b627-5bdb0aafd884" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bfc85a5f-9ea0-5c99-b627-5bdb0aafd884"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfc85a5f-9ea0-5c99-b627-5bdb0aafd884" :answer/id #uuid "bfc85a5f-9ea0-5c99-b627-5bdb0aafd884" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b40650d-078e-5ff0-ae71-e108848a91a7" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b40650d-078e-5ff0-ae71-e108848a91a7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-37ee4f1b-d30b-5342-bae6-dec2a97dfe1f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-37ee4f1b-d30b-5342-bae6-dec2a97dfe1f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37ee4f1b-d30b-5342-bae6-dec2a97dfe1f" :answer/id #uuid "37ee4f1b-d30b-5342-bae6-dec2a97dfe1f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167260 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)-xy=0 \\\\ -4y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.2x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)(1-0.2(2))-(2)y & =0 \\\\ 2.4-2y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1.2).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(5,0),\\,(2,1.2).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164714 :answer-field/correct "answer-4a6dcee2-42ae-52fe-a1fc-f22dd3a0b63c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a6dcee2-42ae-52fe-a1fc-f22dd3a0b63c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a6dcee2-42ae-52fe-a1fc-f22dd3a0b63c" :answer/id #uuid "4a6dcee2-42ae-52fe-a1fc-f22dd3a0b63c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164715 :answer-field/correct "answer-52c89af4-34a4-51e6-a756-47e839363e25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52c89af4-34a4-51e6-a756-47e839363e25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52c89af4-34a4-51e6-a756-47e839363e25" :answer/id #uuid "52c89af4-34a4-51e6-a756-47e839363e25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164716 :answer-field/correct "answer-16b6342b-7e5d-58b5-81a4-338225e24b5e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-16b6342b-7e5d-58b5-81a4-338225e24b5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16b6342b-7e5d-58b5-81a4-338225e24b5e" :answer/id #uuid "16b6342b-7e5d-58b5-81a4-338225e24b5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370395 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.5x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.5x)-xy=0 \\\\ -y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.5x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.5x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)(1-0.5(1))-(1)y & =0 \\\\ 1-y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-bee04d0f-21c8-5845-b5c1-0985dbd842b5" :question/id #uuid "bee04d0f-21c8-5845-b5c1-0985dbd842b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-335629" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=x(1-0.2x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-3y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=x(1-0.2x)-2xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-3y+xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1-0.2x)-2xy)=1-0.4x-2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x(1-0.2x)-2xy)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3y+xy)=y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3y+xy)=-3+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}1-0.4x-2y & -2x \\\\ y & -3+x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}1-0.4(0)-2(0) & -2(0) \\\\ 0 & -3+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d0a4030-1ed3-58e7-b98a-b842cf081e00" "answer-field-06fed757-8210-5ce2-8b39-971f687f871a" "answer-field-8c6f279f-2459-5ccf-a25d-3a9bd5977299" "answer-field-2a87d8b3-fdc8-5111-8645-31b299009bd7" "answer-field-8afe1526-17cc-5b51-bd2c-fc44958a6cdb" "answer-field-6a1bab0c-d263-5a8a-aa46-42456ace7305" "answer-field-517d7ac2-a9a4-59ba-9ce2-95fdac829b49"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d0a4030-1ed3-58e7-b98a-b842cf081e00" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0a4030-1ed3-58e7-b98a-b842cf081e00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-875d6d77-e75c-5729-87b6-f2b11cb97c01" :answer-field/choices ["answer-503cea32-ddf2-5d68-9d83-06424616022c" "answer-1ade26f5-1195-5e01-8e1e-e6fa66791eb0" "answer-3c116c27-bb94-5c96-a6b5-ac7b0be9fc8e" "answer-875d6d77-e75c-5729-87b6-f2b11cb97c01" "answer-102885aa-3ead-52cc-b7ba-326207480dbc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-503cea32-ddf2-5d